{
  "title": "Ciao, Chiara! Che piacere!",
  "translation": "Hi, Chiara! Nice to meet you!",
  "topics": "The alphabet, stress and the sounds c, g, ch, gh, sc",
  "interface": "english",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      "The Italian alphabet has 21 letters: a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i, l, m, n, o, p, q, r, s, t, u, v, z. The letters j, k, w, x and y only appear in foreign words. Italian is almost always read the way it is written: with the few rules below you can read any word.",
      "Sounds are written between two slashes: /k/ is the first sound of *casa*.",
      {
        "h": "Step one: hard and soft vowels"
      },
      "Italian has five vowels. A, o, u are hard; e, i are soft. A soft vowel makes the c, the g or the group sc in front of it soft.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Letters",
            "Before a, o, u: hard sound",
            "Before e, i: soft sound"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "c",
              "/k/: *casa, come, cuore*",
              "/tʃ/: *cena, cinema*"
            ],
            [
              "g",
              "/g/: *gatto, gonna, gusto*",
              "/dʒ/: *gelato, giro*"
            ],
            [
              "sc",
              "/sk/: *scala, sconto, scuola*",
              "/ʃ/: *scena, sci*"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Step two: to keep the sound hard before e or i, add h"
      },
      "The h stands between the consonant and the soft vowel, so the vowel cannot change the sound. The h is never pronounced, here or in any other Italian word.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Spelling",
            "Sound",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "c**h**e, c**h**i",
              "/k/",
              "*che, chilo*"
            ],
            [
              "g**h**e, g**h**i",
              "/g/",
              "*spaghetti, laghi*"
            ],
            [
              "sc**h**e, sc**h**i",
              "/sk/",
              "*scherzo, schiena*"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Step three: to make the sound soft before a, o or u, add i"
      },
      "This i only makes the consonant soft and is not pronounced: *ciao* is read /ˈtʃao/, not /tʃiˈao/. You only hear it when the stress falls on the i itself, as in *farmacia* /farmaˈtʃia/.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Spelling",
            "Sound",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "c**i**a, c**i**o, c**i**u",
              "/tʃ/",
              "*ciao, cioccolato, ciuffo*"
            ],
            [
              "g**i**a, g**i**o, g**i**u",
              "/dʒ/",
              "*giallo, giorno, giusto*"
            ],
            [
              "sc**i**a, sc**i**o, sc**i**u",
              "/ʃ/",
              "*sciarpa, sciopero, sciupare*"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "The whole system"
      },
      "With these three steps every sound can go with every vowel. The highlighted letters are the added h or i.",
      {
        "table": {
          "groups": [
            [
              "",
              1
            ],
            [
              "Hard vowels",
              3
            ],
            [
              "Soft vowels",
              2
            ]
          ],
          "head": [
            "Sound",
            "+ a",
            "+ o",
            "+ u",
            "+ e",
            "+ i"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "/k/",
              "ca",
              "co",
              "cu",
              "c**h**e",
              "c**h**i"
            ],
            [
              "/tʃ/",
              "c**i**a",
              "c**i**o",
              "c**i**u",
              "ce",
              "ci"
            ],
            [
              "/g/",
              "ga",
              "go",
              "gu",
              "g**h**e",
              "g**h**i"
            ],
            [
              "/dʒ/",
              "g**i**a",
              "g**i**o",
              "g**i**u",
              "ge",
              "gi"
            ],
            [
              "/sk/",
              "sca",
              "sco",
              "scu",
              "sc**h**e",
              "sc**h**i"
            ],
            [
              "/ʃ/",
              "sc**i**a",
              "sc**i**o",
              "sc**i**u",
              "sce",
              "sci"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Two more sounds"
      },
      "Gn is always one sound, /ɲ/: *gnocchi, bagno*. Gli is one sound too, /ʎ/: *figli, famiglia*. In *famiglia* the i before the a is again only there for the spelling. Otherwise gl is read as it is written: *inglese*.",
      {
        "h": "Double consonants"
      },
      "Double consonants last longer: *pala* (shovel) and *palla* (ball) are two different words.",
      {
        "h": "Stress"
      },
      "Almost all words are stressed on the second-to-last syllable: **CA**-sa, a-**MI**-co. When the stress falls on the last vowel, it is written with an accent: citt**à**, caff**è**, perch**é**."
    ]
  },
